# Why can't that piece move, and what is behind it?
teaches: ["tactics/pin","tactics/skewer"]
voices: {"a":"gemini:Charon | calm, warm chess coach","b":"gemini:Puck | eager club player, relaxed and good-humoured; never exaggerated"}
prompt: "What is the difference between an absolute and a relative pin? How do you pile up on a pinned piece and win it? How does a skewer differ from a pin?"
next: {"title":"Who is guarding what?","href":"https://www.drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/drawcast-library/chess-30-days/10-who-is-guarding-what.cast"}

## Warm-up: two forks
use: chess_board
with:
    positions:
      - {fen: r2q3k/pp4pp/8/4N3/8/8/PP2QPPP/6K1 w - - 0 1, moves: [Nf7+, Kg8, Nxd8, Rxd8]}
      - {fen: rnbq1rk1/pp2bppp/2p2n2/4N3/8/3P4/PPP2PPP/RNBQKB1R b KQ - 0 1, moves: [Qa5+, Nc3, Qxe5+], flip: true}
    position_shown: 0
    plies_shown: 0

    card title Warm-up

A: Today: why a piece can't move, and what stands behind it. First, two forks from yesterday.
    draw position

A: White to move. Black's king and queen sit one knight jump from the same square.
    highlight piece_h8 piece_d8

B: So which square touches both of them?
    highlight piece_e5

    pause 0.3

    ask "Win material." widget chess line true hints ["A knight move with check.","Which square touches h8 and d8?"] themes ["fork"] voice a teaches ["tactics/fork"]
        right: Knight to f7, check, hits the king and the queen. The king steps aside, the knight takes the queen, and the rook takes the knight.
        wrong: Look for a knight check that also hits the queen.

B: Check first, so Black had no time to save the queen.
    highlight piece_g8

A: Next, you're Black, so the board turns round.
    animate position_shown 1 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

B: White's knight on e5 looks fine. Nothing attacks it.
    highlight piece_e5

A: Nothing attacks it yet. But nothing guards it either.
    point at.ref piece_e5 gesture circle

    pause 0.3

    ask "Win material." widget chess line true hints ["A queen check.","Which queen check also hits e5?"] themes ["fork"] voice a teaches ["tactics/fork"]
        right: Queen to a5, check: the knight blocks on c3, and the queen takes the loose knight on e5, with check.
        wrong: Find a queen check on a line that also runs to e5.

B: Check, a block, and the loose knight drops off.
    highlight piece_e5

A: A fork hits two things side by side. Today's tactics hit two things in a line.
    highlight piece_e5 piece_e1

## Pins and skewers
use: chess_board
with:
    positions:
      - {fen: r2qk2r/ppp2ppp/2np1n2/4p3/1bB1P1b1/2NPBN2/PPP2PPP/R2QK2R w KQkq - 2 7, moves: [Ng5, Bxd1], arrows: [{from: b4, to: e1}, {from: g4, to: d1}]}
      - {fen: r3k2r/pp1b1ppp/3p4/4n3/8/2P5/PP1B1PPP/RN2R1K1 w - - 0 1, moves: [Rxe5+, dxe5], arrows: [{from: e1, to: e8}]}
      - {fen: r3k2r/pp1b1ppp/3p4/4n3/8/2P5/PP1B1PPP/RN2R1K1 w - - 0 1, moves: [f4, f6, fxe5, fxe5], arrows: [{from: e1, to: e8}]}
      - {fen: r7/p4ppp/1pk5/8/8/3B4/PP3PPP/6K1 w - - 0 1, moves: [Be4+, Kd6, Bxa8], arrows: [{from: e4, to: a8}]}
    position_shown: 0
    plies_shown: 0
sources:
    - id: pin
      title: Pin (chess)
      authors: Wikipedia contributors
      year: 2026
      finding: An absolute pin is to the king, so the pinned piece may not legally move; in a relative pin the piece may move but exposes a more valuable piece; a pinned piece should be attacked again, often by a pawn.
      url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pin_(chess)
    - id: skewer
      title: Skewer (chess)
      authors: Wikipedia contributors
      year: 2026
      finding: A skewer attacks two pieces in a line with the more valuable one in front; it is sometimes called a reverse pin.
      url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Skewer_(chess)
    - id: reinfeld
      title: The Chess Pin (Chess Notes)
      authors: Edward Winter
      year: 2024
      finding: '''The pin is mightier than the sword'' is Fred Reinfeld''s remark, quoted in his Treasury of Chess Lore (1951) and attributed to him in Winning Chess (1948).'
      url: https://www.chesshistory.com/winter/extra/pin.html

    card title "Pins and skewers"

A: Here's a quiet opening, and two black bishops aim straight through White's knights.
    draw position

A: Bishop takes f7 is check, but the king just takes it: a bishop for a pawn.
    highlight piece_c4

A: The bishop on b4 looks through the knight on c3, at the king.
    draw arrow_0

B: Knight to d5, then. It hits that bishop.
    highlight piece_c3

A: You can't. Move that knight and your own king is in check. That's an absolute pin: the piece can't move at all.
    highlight piece_e1

A: The other bishop looks through the f3 knight, at the queen.
    draw arrow_1

    erase arrow_0

B: Then knight to g5, attacking f7.
    animate plies_shown 1 duration 1.3

A: That one's legal, and Black takes your queen.
    animate plies_shown 2 duration 1.3

    erase arrow_1

A: That's a relative pin: the knight may move, but what stands behind it falls.
    highlight piece_d1

A: A piece that can't run is a target.
    animate position_shown 1 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

A: Here White's rook pins the knight to the king.
    draw arrow_0

B: Rook takes e5, check? It can't get away.
    animate plies_shown 1 duration 1.3

A: Count it first. The pawn takes back: you've given a rook for a knight.
    animate plies_shown 2 duration 1.3

    erase arrow_0

A: The American chess writer Fred Reinfeld put it in one line:
    animate position_shown 2 plies_shown 0 duration 0.6

A: the pin is mightier than the sword.
    draw arrow_0

A: It's pinned, so there's no hurry. Pile up: attack it again, with your cheapest piece.
    highlight piece_e5

    pause 0.3

    ask "Win the pinned knight." widget chess line 1 hints ["Attack it once more.","Your cheapest piece is a pawn."] themes ["pin"] voice a
        right: Pawn to f4: the knight is attacked by a pawn and can't step away.
        wrong: Which of your pieces is cheapest, and which one can attack e5?

B: Black guards it again.
    animate plies_shown 2 duration 1.3

A: Doesn't matter. Your pawn takes it: a pawn for a knight.
    animate plies_shown 4 duration 2.4

    erase arrow_0

A: Now turn a pin round.
    animate position_shown 3 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

A: Black's king stands in front, the rook behind it.
    highlight piece_c6 piece_a8

    pause 0.3

    ask "Win material." widget chess line true hints ["A bishop check.","Check the king along the line to a8."] themes ["skewer"] voice a
        right: Bishop to e4, check: the king must step off the line, and the bishop takes the rook on a8.
        wrong: Give a check on the long diagonal through c6.

B: The king had to step away, and the rook behind it fell.
    highlight piece_a8

A: That's a skewer.
    animate position_shown 3 plies_shown 1 duration 1

A: In a pin the smaller piece is in front; in a skewer, the bigger one.
    draw arrow_0

B: So a pin freezes a piece, and a skewer chases it away.
    point at.ref piece_c6 gesture circle

A: So look along every line that runs through a king, a queen or a rook.
    highlight piece_c6 piece_a8

## Your turn: pins and skewers
feedback:
    style: dry
    perfect: Six for six. Nothing on a line is safe from you.
    good: Most of them. The lines will start to jump out at you.
    poor: Lines hide well at first. Look through every king and queen, and try again.
    none: The puzzles will wait for you.
use: chess_board
with:
    positions:
      - {fen: r5k1/pp4pp/5p2/3q4/8/1P3N2/P4PPP/4RBK1 w - - 0 1, moves: [Bc4, Qxc4, bxc4], arrows: [{from: c4, to: g8}]}
      - {fen: r2qk2r/1p3ppp/2np1n2/pB6/3P4/5N2/PPP2PPP/R2Q1RK1 w kq - 0 1, moves: [d5], arrows: [{from: b5, to: e8}]}
      - {fen: 6k1/pp2bppp/8/8/8/4K3/PP4PP/6R1 b - - 0 1, moves: [Bc5+, Kd3, Bxg1], flip: true}
      - {fen: rn1qkbnr/ppp2ppp/4p3/3p4/6b1/2P1PN2/PP1P1PPP/RNBQKB1R w KQkq - 0 1, moves: [Qa4+, Nc6, Qxg4]}
      - {fen: r1bq1rk1/1pp2ppp/2n2n2/p2p4/Pb6/2NP4/1PP1BPPP/R1BQK1NR b KQ - 0 1, moves: [d4], flip: true, arrows: [{from: b4, to: e1}]}
      - {fen: 8/5p1p/1q3k2/8/8/7R/5PPP/6K1 w - - 0 1, moves: [Rh6+, Kg5, Rxb6]}
    position_shown: 0
    plies_shown: 0

    card title "Your turn"

A: Now you've seen pins and skewers: your turn. Six puzzles, with one fork from yesterday mixed in.
    draw position

    pause 0.3

    ask "Win material." widget chess line true hints ["Black's queen and king share a diagonal.","Your bishop on f1."] themes ["pin"] voice a
        right: Bishop to c4 pins the queen to her king. Her best is to take the bishop, and the pawn takes her back.
        wrong: Look along the diagonal from Black's king through the queen.

    animate plies_shown 1 duration 1

B: The queen can't leave the line, because her king is behind her.
    draw arrow_0

    erase arrow_0

A: Next: one black knight here can't move.
    animate position_shown 1 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

A: Make it pay.
    draw arrow_0

    pause 0.3

    ask "Win a piece." widget chess line true hints ["The knight on c6 is pinned to its king.","Attack it with a pawn."] themes ["pin"] voice a
        right: Pawn to d5 hits the pinned knight. It can't step away, so a pawn wins a piece.
        wrong: The knight on c6 can't move. What is the cheapest way to attack it?

    erase arrow_0

A: Now you're Black, so the board turns round.
    animate position_shown 2 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

    pause 0.3

    ask "Win material." widget chess line true hints ["White's king and rook share a diagonal.","A bishop check."] themes ["skewer"] voice a
        right: Bishop to c5, check: the king steps off the diagonal, and the bishop takes the rook on g1.
        wrong: Find a check on the line that runs from the king to the rook.

B: King in front, rook behind. A skewer.
    highlight piece_g1

A: Back to White, and yesterday's theme.
    animate position_shown 3 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

    pause 0.3

    ask "Win material." widget chess line true hints ["Which black piece is loose?","A queen check."] themes ["fork"] voice a teaches ["tactics/fork"]
        right: Queen to a4, check: a queen fork. However Black answers the check, here with the knight, the loose bishop on g4 drops off.
        wrong: Find the black piece with no guard, then a queen check that also hits it.

A: Black again.
    animate position_shown 4 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

A: One white knight is frozen.
    draw arrow_0

    pause 0.3

    ask "Win a piece." widget chess line true hints ["The knight on c3 is pinned to its king.","Attack it with a pawn."] themes ["pin"] voice a
        right: Pawn to d4 hits the pinned knight. It can't step away, so a pawn wins a piece.
        wrong: Which white piece can't move? Attack it with your cheapest piece.

    erase arrow_0

A: Last one: White to move.
    animate position_shown 5 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

    pause 0.3

    ask "Win material." widget chess line true hints ["Black's king and queen share a rank.","A rook check."] themes ["skewer"] voice a
        right: Rook to h6, check: the king steps aside, and the rook takes the queen.
        wrong: Find a check along the sixth rank.

B: The king had to step aside, and the queen went.
    highlight piece_b6

A: Before every move, look along the lines through kings, queens and rooks: what's in front, and what's behind?
    highlight piece_g5 piece_b6

A: Homework: one slow game today, fifteen plus ten or slower. Then find its first mistake.
    erase position

## Where next
